# Suppose you could draw 1,000 different random samples from the same population and compute beta1-hat in each one. If OLS is unbiased (SLR1-SLR4 hold), which statement is correct?
# ANSWER: The average of the 1,000 estimates is close to beta1.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Under SLR1-SLR5, Var(beta1-hat) = sigma^2 / SST_x. Holding everything else fixed, which changes would make Var(beta1-hat) larger? Select all that apply.
# ANSWER (if it says LARGER):
# - A smaller sample (fewer observations with X spread the same way, so a smaller SST_x)  -> check
# - A larger true slope beta1                                                              -> leave blank
# - A larger error variance sigma^2                                                        -> check
# - X values bunched more tightly together (a smaller SST_x)                               -> check
# - A larger sample (more observations with X spread the same way, so a larger SST_x)      -> leave blank
#
# ANSWER (if it says SMALLER instead):
# - A larger sample (more observations with X spread the same way, so a larger SST_x)      -> check (only one)
# - Everything else -> leave blank (smaller sample, larger sigma^2, and bunched X all make it larger;
#   the true slope beta1 has no effect on the variance)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A simple regression on n = 8 observations has sigma-hat = 6.8, and the sample standard deviation of X is S_x = 2.8. Find the standard error se(beta1-hat). (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: SST_x = (n - 1) * S_x^2 (square the standard deviation), then se(beta1-hat) = sigma-hat / sqrt(SST_x).
n         <- 8      # edit
sigma_hat <- 6.8    # edit
s_x       <- 2.8    # edit
sst_x <- (n - 1) * s_x^2
round(sigma_hat / sqrt(sst_x), 2)  # 0.92

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Under SLR1-SLR5, a regression has sd(beta1-hat) = 2.03. If the sample were 6 times as large, with the X values spread out in the same way (so SST_x is also 6 times as large), what would sd(beta1-hat) be? (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: sd(beta1-hat) = sigma / sqrt(SST_x). Multiplying SST_x by k divides the sd by sqrt(k), not by k.
sd_old <- 2.03      # edit
k      <- 6         # edit
round(sd_old / sqrt(k), 2)         # 0.83

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A multiple regression gives y-hat = -1 + 0.9 x1 + 2.3 x2. Find the predicted value y-hat at x1 = 5 and x2 = 20. (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: plug both values in, exactly as in simple regression: y-hat = beta0-hat + beta1-hat x1 + beta2-hat x2.
b0 <- -1            # edit
b1 <- 0.9           # edit
b2 <- 2.3           # edit
x1 <- 5             # edit
x2 <- 20            # edit
round(b0 + b1 * x1 + b2 * x2, 2)   # 49.50

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A multiple regression gives y-hat = 6 + 2.9 x1 - 1.1 x2. If x1 rises by 4 and x2 rises by 2, by how much does y-hat change? (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: change in y-hat = beta1-hat * change in x1 + beta2-hat * change in x2. Here beta2-hat is negative, and the intercept does not matter.
b1  <- 2.9          # edit
b2  <- -1.1         # edit
dx1 <- 4            # edit
dx2 <- 2            # edit
round(b1 * dx1 + b2 * dx2, 2)      # 9.40

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data: wage1 (wage = average hourly earnings in dollars; educ = years of education; exper = years of experience; tenure = years with the current employer). Run reg <- lm(wage ~ educ + exper + tenure, data = wage1). What is the predicted hourly wage of a worker with 14 years of education, 10 years of experience and 5 years of tenure? (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: e.g. save the coefficients with b <- reg$coefficients, then do something like b[1] + b[2]*14 + b[3]*10 + b[4]*5.
data("wage1")
reg <- lm(wage ~ educ + exper + tenure, data = wage1)
b <- reg$coefficients
educ_val   <- 14    # edit
exper_val  <- 10    # edit
tenure_val <- 5     # edit
round(unname(b[1] + b[2] * educ_val + b[3] * exper_val + b[4] * tenure_val), 2)  # 6.58

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data: wage1 (wage = average hourly earnings in dollars; educ = years of education; exper = years of experience; tenure = years with the current employer). Using the same regression of wage on educ, exper and tenure, by how much does the predicted hourly wage change when tenure rises by 3 years, holding education and experience fixed? (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: change in wage-hat = beta3-hat * change in tenure when educ and exper do not change.
delta_tenure <- 3   # edit
round(unname(b[4] * delta_tenure), 2)  # 0.51
